The correct form, executed:

```go
func reverse(s string) string {
    r := []rune(s)
    for i, j := 0, len(r)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        r[i], r[j] = r[j], r[i]
    }
    return string(r)
}
// reverse("hello") → "olleh" — verified
```

The trap: byte-reversal corrupts non-ASCII:
```go
b := []byte("héllo")
// swap bytes → é's two bytes (195, 169) become (169, 195) — invalid UTF-8
```

The mechanics: "héllo" is 6 bytes (é is 2). []rune decodes it into 5 code points. Reversing RUNES moves each CHARACTER as a unit. Byte reversal splits the rune — the output is garbage. The strings topic covers the full UTF-8 story.

The costs: []rune(s) allocates and decodes (O(n)); the swap is O(n/2); string(r) re-encodes (O(n)). Three passes — the honest cost of Unicode correctness.
